Keep the OSCrypt key in HUKS.

PosixKeyProvider was the only provider here, and its key is a constant
compiled into every Chromium build, so Login Data was obfuscated rather than
encrypted: anyone holding the file could read it offline, without the device.

HUKS keys cannot be exported and OSCrypt's KeyProvider has to hand its key
over for in-process use, so the two do not meet directly. Chromium's key is
generated here and stored wrapped -- HUKS holds a key that never leaves the
secure world, and that key encrypts ours. What lands on disk is useless
without this device and this app, which is the bargain Keychain and DPAPI
strike on macOS and Windows.

It does not keep the key out of process memory. Doing that means encrypting
every value through HUKS, which is a change to OSCrypt's interface rather than
an implementation of it. That trade was agreed before this was written.

PosixKeyProvider stays registered underneath so values written before this can
still be read; new values are written with the HUKS-wrapped key, under its own
tag. Failure to reach HUKS reports kTemporarilyUnavailable rather than
kPermanentlyUnavailable, because the latter invites callers to treat existing
data as lost.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

